    "title": "Worked Benchmark: Chernoff vs Chebyshev on Coin Flips",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-30606\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30606)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-30606)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">n races</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">bound on \u2119(S\u2099 \u2265 \u00ben)</text><path d=\"M28.0,76.4 L30.5,83.8 L33.0,88.7 L35.4,92.1 L37.9,94.7 L40.4,96.6 L42.9,98.2 L45.3,99.5 L47.8,100.5 L50.3,101.4 L52.8,102.2 L55.2,102.9 L57.7,103.4 L60.2,103.9 L62.6,104.4 L65.1,104.8 L67.6,105.2 L70.1,105.5 L72.5,105.8 L75.0,106.1 L77.5,106.3 L80.0,106.5 L82.5,106.8 L84.9,107.0 L87.4,107.1 L89.9,107.3 L92.3,107.5 L94.8,107.6 L97.3,107.7 L99.8,107.9 L102.2,108.0 L104.7,108.1 L107.2,108.2 L109.7,108.3 L112.2,108.4 L114.6,108.5 L117.1,108.6 L119.6,108.7 L122.0,108.8 L124.5,108.8 L127.0,108.9 L129.5,109.0 L131.9,109.0 L134.4,109.1 L136.9,109.2 L139.4,109.2 L141.8,109.3 L144.3,109.3 L146.8,109.4 L149.3,109.4 L151.8,109.5 L154.2,109.5 L156.7,109.6 L159.2,109.6 L161.7,109.7 L164.1,109.7 L166.6,109.7 L169.1,109.8 L171.6,109.8 L174.0,109.8 L176.5,109.9 L179.0,109.9 L181.4,109.9 L183.9,110.0 L186.4,110.0 L188.9,110.0 L191.3,110.1 L193.8,110.1 L196.3,110.1 L198.8,110.1 L201.2,110.2 L203.7,110.2 L206.2,110.2 L208.7,110.2 L211.2,110.3 L213.6,110.3 L216.1,110.3 L218.6,110.3 L221.1,110.3 L223.5,110.4 L226.0,110.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">4/n Chebyshev</text><path d=\"M24.0,28.0 L27.4,34.8 L30.7,41.0 L34.1,46.7 L37.5,52.0 L40.8,56.9 L44.2,61.3 L47.6,65.4 L50.9,69.2 L54.3,72.6 L57.7,75.8 L61.0,78.7 L64.4,81.4 L67.8,83.9 L71.1,86.1 L74.5,88.2 L77.9,90.2 L81.2,91.9 L84.6,93.5 L88.0,95.0 L91.3,96.4 L94.7,97.7 L98.1,98.8 L101.4,99.9 L104.8,100.9 L108.2,101.8 L111.5,102.6 L114.9,103.3 L118.3,104.0 L121.6,104.7 L125.0,105.3 L128.4,105.8 L131.7,106.3 L135.1,106.8 L138.5,107.2 L141.8,107.6 L145.2,107.9 L148.6,108.3 L151.9,108.6 L155.3,108.8 L158.7,109.1 L162.0,109.3 L165.4,109.6 L168.8,109.7 L172.1,109.9 L175.5,110.1 L178.9,110.3 L182.2,110.4 L185.6,110.5 L189.0,110.6 L192.3,110.8 L195.7,110.9 L199.1,110.9 L202.4,111.0 L205.8,111.1 L209.2,111.2 L212.5,111.2 L215.9,111.3 L219.3,111.4 L222.6,111.4 L226.0,111.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">e^(\u2212n/8) Chernoff</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">the exponential bound wins as the races pile up</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The bounds on \u2119(S\u2099 \u2265 \u00ben) as the number of races n grows. The Chebyshev bound 4/n falls slowly, the Chernoff bound e^(\u2212n/8) (dashed) falls exponentially.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Problem Setup:</strong> Let $S_n \\sim \\text{Binomial}(n, 1/2)$, so $\\mu = n/2$ and $\\sigma^2 = n/4$. Bound tail $\\mathbb{P}(S_n \\ge \\frac{3}{4}n)$ ($\\ge 25\\%$ excess).",
      "<strong>Chebyshev Evaluation:</strong> $\\mathbb{P}(S_n - n/2 \\ge n/4) \\le \\frac{\\sigma^2}{(n/4)^2} = \\frac{n/4}{n^2/16} = \\frac{4}{n}$ (slow polynomial rate $O(1/n)$).",
      "<strong>Chernoff Evaluation:</strong> Hoeffding gives $\\exp(-2(n/4)^2 / n) = \\exp(-n/8)$. For $n=100$: Chebyshev gives $\\le 0.04$, while Chernoff gives $\\le e^{-12.5} \\approx 3.7 \\times 10^{-6}$!"
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{P}\\left(S_n \\ge \\tfrac{3}{4}n\\right) \\le \\frac{4}{n} \\quad (\\text{Chebyshev}) \\quad \\text{vs} \\quad \\exp\\left(-\\frac{n}{8}\\right) \\quad (\\text{Chernoff/Hoeffding})$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>How rare is a freak season?</strong> For a driver who wins half his races, after 100 races Chebyshev says winning three quarters of them has chance at most 4%, while the exponential bound says about one in a quarter of a million. More information gives a much sharper ceiling, which is why runs like Max's 2023 season say the car is genuinely faster.",
    "examTrap": "Using Chebyshev for large sample sizes (n >> 100). Chebyshev captures only 2nd moments, missing the exponential destructive cancellation across independent terms.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Chebyshev against Chernoff.</b> Let $S_n$ be the number of heads in $n$ tosses of a fair coin. Chebyshev gives $\\mathbb P(S_n\\ge\\tfrac34n)\\le\\mathbb P(|S_n-\\tfrac n2|\\ge\\tfrac n4)\\le\\dfrac{n/4}{(n/4)^2}=\\dfrac4n$. Hoeffding (Chernoff) gives $\\mathbb P(S_n\\ge\\tfrac34n)\\le\\exp\\!\\bigl(-\\tfrac{2(n/4)^2}{n}\\bigr)=e^{-n/8}$. The second decays exponentially, the first only like $1/n$ (Ross 8.2; Grimmett 7.3).",
    "hint": "Over a long run of races, which bound on a freak run of wins shrinks faster: the 1/n or the exponential one?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathbb P(S_n\\ge\\tfrac34n)$ = a freak run.</strong> Over $n$ races, $S_n$ counts the wins of a driver who wins each race with chance $\\tfrac12$; the event is far more wins than the half you expect.",
      "<strong>$4/n$ = Chebyshev, from the variance alone.</strong> It uses only the mean and spread, so it falls slowly, like one over the number of races.",
      "<strong>$e^{-n/8}$ = Chernoff/Hoeffding, using the whole MGF.</strong> It exploits that independent races partly cancel, and shrinks exponentially."
    ],
