    "id": 31005,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "10_Exchangeable_Random_Variables",
    "topicTitle": "10 \u00b7 Exchangeable Random Variables",
    "cardNumber": "05",
    "category": "DE FINETTI THEOREM",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "De Finetti\u2019s Representation Theorem: Conditionally i.i.d. Mixtures",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31005\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"36\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u0398: this car's hidden win chance</text><line x1=\"120.0\" y1=\"41.0\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31005)\"/><line x1=\"120.0\" y1=\"41.0\" x2=\"120.0\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31005)\"/><line x1=\"120.0\" y1=\"41.0\" x2=\"180.0\" y2=\"70.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31005)\"/><rect x=\"42\" y=\"70\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2081</text><rect x=\"102\" y=\"70\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2082</text><rect x=\"162\" y=\"70\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">X\u2083</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">given \u0398 = \u03b8: races are i.i.d. Bernoulli(\u03b8)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2119(X\u2081=x\u2081\u2026X\u2099=x\u2099) = \u222b \u03b8^(\u2211x\u1d62)(1\u2212\u03b8)^(n\u2212\u2211x\u1d62) dF(\u03b8)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"136\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u0398 = long-run share of wins</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A hidden win chance \u0398 points at the races X\u2081, X\u2082, X\u2083. Given \u0398 = \u03b8 they are independent Bernoulli(\u03b8) flips, and the joint law averages that product over the law of \u0398.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Representation Statement:</strong> Let $(X_1, X_2, \\dots)$ be an infinite exchangeable sequence of binary variables $X_i \\in \\{0, 1\\}$. Then there exists a unique distribution function $F_\\Theta$ on $[0, 1]$ such that given $\\Theta = \\theta$, $X_i$ are i.i.d. $\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$.",
      "<strong>Integral Mixture:</strong> $\\mathbb{P}(X_1 = x_1, \\dots, X_n = x_n) = \\int_0^1 \\theta^{\\sum x_i} (1 - \\theta)^{n - \\sum x_i} dF_\\Theta(\\theta)$.",
      "<strong>Bayesian Foundation:</strong> Justifies Bayesian probability: the 'unknown parameter' $\\Theta$ is mathematically proven to exist as the almost sure limiting frequency $\\Theta = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\mathbb{P}(X_1=x_1, \\dots, X_n=x_n) = \\int_0^1 \\theta^{\\sum x_i} (1-\\theta)^{n - \\sum x_i}\\,dF_\\Theta(\\theta), \\quad \\Theta = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>How good is this year's car?</strong> Before you know how fast the car is, one win tells you something about the next race. If you knew the car's true win chance, races would be independent coin flips. Not knowing it makes the races exchangeable but linked.",
    "examTrap": "Applying De Finetti to finite sequences! De Finetti FAILS for finite exchangeable collections; the infinite horizon is strictly necessary for the representation integral to hold.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>de Finetti's theorem.</b> Let $X_1,X_2,\\ldots\\in\\{0,1\\}$ be an infinite exchangeable sequence. Then there is a random variable $\\Theta\\in[0,1]$, $\\Theta=\\lim_n\\frac1n\\sum_{i\\le n}X_i$ a.s., with law $F_\\Theta$, such that $\\mathbb P(X_1=x_1,\\ldots,X_n=x_n)=\\int_0^1\\theta^{\\sum x_i}(1-\\theta)^{n-\\sum x_i}\\,dF_\\Theta(\\theta)$: conditionally on $\\Theta$, the $X_i$ are i.i.d. Bernoulli($\\Theta$) (Grimmett 7.2; Durrett 4.1).",
    "hint": "Is an exchangeable record of wins and losses secretly independent races with a hidden win chance?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\Theta$ = a hidden win chance.</strong> Say how good this season's Red Bull car really is: fixed once and for all but unknown to you, and equal to the long-run share of races Max wins.",
      "<strong>$\\theta^{\\sum x_i}(1-\\theta)^{n-\\sum x_i}$ = i.i.d. races given $\\Theta=\\theta$.</strong> Given the hidden chance, each race is an independent win/no-win coin flip.",
      "<strong>$\\int\\ldots dF_\\Theta(\\theta)$ = average over the unknown chance.</strong> Mixing over what $\\Theta$ might be gives the exchangeable record: dependent races are independent races with a common hidden cause."
    ],
    "textbookImg": null,
