    "id": 31403,
    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "14_Random_Sums_and_Walds_Equation",
    "topicTitle": "14 \u00b7 Random Sums & Wald\u2019s Identity",
    "cardNumber": "03",
    "category": "WALD IDENTITIES",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-emerald",
    "title": "Wald\u2019s Second Identity: Two-Source Variance Decomposition",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31403\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><rect x=\"30\" y=\"56\" width=\"180\" height=\"24\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"56\" width=\"100\" height=\"24\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"56\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[N] Var(X)</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">(\ud835\udd3cX)\u00b2 Var(N)</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">noise within each stop</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">noise in how many stops</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">Var(S) = \ud835\udd3c[N] Var(X) + (\ud835\udd3c[X])\u00b2 Var(N)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">two sources of randomness: stop times and stop count</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A bar for Var(S) split into \ud835\udd3c[N]Var(X), the variation in each stop's time, and (\ud835\udd3c[X])\u00b2Var(N), the variation in how many stops there are.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Identity Statement:</strong> If $N$ is independent of i.i.d. $X_i$ with finite variances, $\\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\mathbb{E}[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X) + (\\mathbb{E}[X])^2 \\operatorname{Var}(N)$.",
      "<strong>Derivation via Total Variance:</strong> $\\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(S_N \\mid N)] + \\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[S_N \\mid N]) = \\mathbb{E}[N\\sigma^2] + \\operatorname{Var}(N\\mu) = \\mathbb{E}[N]\\sigma^2 + \\mu^2 \\operatorname{Var}(N)$.",
      "<strong>Zero-Mean Simplification:</strong> If $\\mathbb{E}[X] = 0$, the second term vanishes: $\\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\mathbb{E}[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ (holds even when $N$ is a general stopping time)."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\operatorname{Var}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N X_i\\right) = \\mathbb{E}[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X) + \\left(\\mathbb{E}[X]\\right)^2 \\operatorname{Var}(N)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Two reasons races differ.</strong> Some races differ because a stop goes well or badly (a sticky wheel nut); others because Max stops once instead of twice. The total variation adds both reasons.",
    "examTrap": "Using Var(S_N) = \ud835\udd3c[N]Var(X)! Omitting the second term \u03bc\u00b2 Var(N) severely underestimates risk whenever mean \ud835\udd3c[X] is non-zero.",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Wald's second identity.</b> If $N$ is independent of the i.i.d. $X_i$ with finite second moments, then $\\operatorname{Var}\\bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^NX_i\\bigr)=\\mathbb E[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X)+(\\mathbb E[X])^2\\operatorname{Var}(N)$ (Ross 7.5; Grimmett 3.7).",
    "hint": "With a random number of pit stops, where do the two sources of randomness show up in the variance of the total time in the pits?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ = randomness of each stop's time.</strong> On average $\\mathbb E[N]$ stops, each adding its own variance.",
      "<strong>$(\\mathbb E[X])^2\\operatorname{Var}(N)$ = randomness in how many stops.</strong> One extra stop shifts the total by $\\mathbb E[X]$, so the count's variance is scaled by $(\\mathbb E[X])^2$.",
      "<strong>The sum = Eve's law.</strong> $\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}(S\\mid N)]=\\mathbb E[N]\\operatorname{Var}(X)$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E[S\\mid N])=\\operatorname{Var}(N\\mathbb E[X])$."
    ],
