    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Worked Benchmark: Compound Poisson Claims (The Lundberg Model)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31406\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31406)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31406)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">time</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">lead lost S(t)</text><line x1=\"34\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"68\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"82\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"68\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"82\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"104\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"104\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"196\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"196\" y1=\"16\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"16\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">Poisson(\u03bb) safety cars,</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">sizes X\u1d62 ~ Exp(\u03b2)</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\ud835\udd3c[S] = \u03bb \ud835\udd3c[X]</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">Var(S) = \u03bb \ud835\udd3c[X\u00b2] = 2\u03bb/\u03b2\u00b2</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"6.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">variance of a compound Poisson uses the SECOND moment of the jumps</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The lead lost S(t) jumps by X\u1d62 at each safety car, which arrive as a Poisson stream. Its mean is \u03bb\ud835\udd3c[X] and its variance \u03bb\ud835\udd3c[X\u00b2], which equals 2\u03bb/\u03b2\u00b2 for exponential jump sizes.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Problem Setup:</strong> Claim count $N \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\lambda)$ (so $\\mathbb{E}[N] = \\lambda, \\operatorname{Var}(N) = \\lambda$). Individual claim amounts $X_i \\stackrel{\\text{i.i.d.}}{\\sim} \\text{Exp}(\\beta)$ with mean $1/\\beta$ and variance $1/\\beta^2$.",
      "<strong>Expected Total Claim:</strong> $\\mathbb{E}[S_N] = \\mathbb{E}[N]\\mathbb{E}[X] = \\lambda \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\beta} = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\beta}$.",
      "<strong>Aggregate Variance:</strong> $\\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\lambda(1/\\beta^2) + (1/\\beta)^2 \\lambda = \\frac{2\\lambda}{\\beta^2} = \\lambda \\mathbb{E}[X^2]$. For ANY compound Poisson sum, $\\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\lambda \\mathbb{E}[X^2]$!"
    ],
    "math": "$$N \\sim \\text{Pois}(\\lambda) \\implies \\mathbb{E}[S_N] = \\lambda \\mathbb{E}[X], \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(S_N) = \\lambda \\mathbb{E}[X^2] = \\frac{2\\lambda}{\\beta^2}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Safety cars eat the lead.</strong> Safety cars come out at random during the race, and each one bunches the field up and costs Max a random number of seconds of his lead. The total lost has an average of rate times average cost, and a spread driven by the big losses.",
    "examTrap": "Omitting the count variance term: calculating Var(S_N) = \u03bb / \u03b2\u00b2 by treating N as a constant! Because Var(N) = \u03bb, the second term adds another \u03bb/\u03b2\u00b2, exactly doubling the variance.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>Compound Poisson (Lundberg) model.</b> Let $N\\sim\\mathrm{Poisson}(\\lambda)$ be independent of i.i.d. $X_i$ (for example $X_i\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(\\beta)$) and $S=\\sum_{i=1}^NX_i$. Then $\\mathbb E[S]=\\lambda\\,\\mathbb E[X]$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(S)=\\lambda\\,\\mathbb E[X^2]$, which equals $\\dfrac{2\\lambda}{\\beta^2}$ for exponential claims (Ross 7.5; Grimmett 6.8).",
    "hint": "Safety cars come out as a Poisson stream during a race, each wiping out a random chunk of Max's lead. What are the mean and variance of the total lead lost?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\mathbb E[S]=\\lambda\\mathbb E[X]$ = rate times mean size.</strong> The average number of safety cars times the average lead lost to one.",
      "<strong>$\\operatorname{Var}(S)=\\lambda\\mathbb E[X^2]$ = rate times the second moment.</strong> Wald II with $\\mathbb E[N]=\\operatorname{Var}(N)=\\lambda$ gives $\\lambda\\operatorname{Var}X+\\lambda(\\mathbb EX)^2$.",
      "<strong>$2\\lambda/\\beta^2$ = exponential sizes.</strong> For $X\\sim\\mathrm{Exp}(\\beta)$, $\\mathbb E[X^2]=2/\\beta^2$, so the rare huge losses drive the variance."
    ],
