    "cardNumber": "06",
    "category": "WORKED BENCHMARK",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Worked Benchmark: 1D Random Walk Recurrence (P\u00f3lya\u2019s Theorem)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-31906\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31906)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-31906)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">lap n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">gap to rival</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><path d=\"M30.0,60.0 L38.0,70.0 L46.0,60.0 L54.0,70.0 L62.0,80.0 L70.0,70.0 L78.0,60.0 L86.0,50.0 L94.0,40.0 L102.0,50.0 L110.0,60.0 L118.0,50.0 L126.0,40.0 L134.0,50.0 L142.0,60.0 L150.0,50.0 L158.0,40.0 L166.0,50.0 L174.0,40.0 L182.0,30.0 L190.0,40.0 L198.0,50.0 L206.0,40.0 L214.0,30.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">back at 0 only at even n</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">P\u2080\u2080\u207d\u00b2\u207f\u207e ~ (4p(1\u2212p))\u207f/\u221a(\u03c0n):  p = \u00bd \u2192 \u2211 = \u221e (recurrent)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">p \u2260 \u00bd \u2192 geometric decay, \u2211 &lt; \u221e (transient)</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The gap to a rival as a random walk, one step per lap, with level 0 dashed. With p = \u00bd the return chances ~ 1/\u221a(\u03c0n) add up to infinity; with p \u2260 \u00bd they shrink geometrically and the sum is finite.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Problem Setup:</strong> Random walk on $\\mathbb{Z}$: $X_{n+1} = X_n + \\xi_n$ where $\\mathbb{P}(\\xi = +1) = p$ and $\\mathbb{P}(\\xi = -1) = 1 - p$. Returns occur only at even steps $2n$.",
      "<strong>Return Probability:</strong> $P_{00}^{(2n)} = \\binom{2n}{n} p^n (1-p)^n$. By Stirling's approximation, $\\binom{2n}{n} \\sim \\frac{4^n}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}$, so $P_{00}^{(2n)} \\sim \\frac{(4p(1-p))^n}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}$.",
      "<strong>Dichotomy:</strong> If $p = 1/2$, $4p(1-p) = 1$, so $\\sum P_{00}^{(2n)} \\sim \\sum \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}} = \\infty \\implies$ **RECURRENT**. If $p \\ne 1/2$, $4p(1-p) \\lt  1$, so the series converges geometrically $\\implies$ **TRANSIENT**."
    ],
    "math": "$$P_{00}^{(2n)} \\sim \\frac{(4p(1-p))^n}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}} \\implies \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty P_{00}^{(2n)} = \\begin{cases} \\infty & p = 1/2 \\quad (\\text{Recurrent}) \\\\ \\lt  \\infty & p \\ne 1/2 \\quad (\\text{Transient}) \\end{cases}$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>The gap to a rival.</strong> Each lap Max either gains a tenth on the rival or loses one. If both cars are equally quick, the gap comes back to zero for sure, again and again. If Max's car is faster, the gap drifts away and may never be zero again.",
    "examTrap": "Missing period 2: P_{00}^{(2n+1)} = 0 for all odd steps. Returns require an equal number of left and right steps; only even powers contribute to the return series.",
    "stmtKind": "WORKED EXAMPLE",
    "statement": "<b>P\u00f3lya: the 1D random walk.</b> Step $+1$ with probability $p$, $-1$ with $1-p$. Then $P^{(2n)}_{00}=\\binom{2n}n(p(1-p))^n\\sim\\frac{(4p(1-p))^n}{\\sqrt{\\pi n}}$. For $p=\\tfrac12$ the sum $\\sum_nP^{(2n)}_{00}=\\infty$ (recurrent); for $p\\ne\\tfrac12$ it is finite (transient) (Ross 4.3; Grimmett 6.3).",
    "hint": "If the gap to a rival goes up or down one step each lap, are they sure to be level again? What if Max's car is faster?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$P^{(2n)}_{00}=\\binom{2n}n(p(1-p))^n$ = $n$ ups and $n$ downs.</strong> Getting back to a zero gap needs as many steps up as down, so only at an even lap count.",
      "<strong>$\\sim(4p(1-p))^n/\\sqrt{\\pi n}$ = Stirling.</strong> For $p=\\frac12$ the base is exactly 1, leaving $1/\\sqrt{\\pi n}$; otherwise $4p(1-p)\\lt 1$.",
      "<strong>$\\sum=\\infty$ iff $p=\\frac12$.</strong> $\\sum1/\\sqrt n$ diverges (recurrent); a geometric tail adds up to a finite number (transient)."
    ],
