    "highlighterClass": "highlight-amber",
    "title": "Fundamental Limit Theorem & Return Time Reciprocal",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32002\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32002)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32002)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">race n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2119(win in race n)</text><path d=\"M24.0,48.0 L28.0,49.9 L32.1,51.7 L36.1,53.2 L40.2,54.6 L44.2,55.9 L48.2,57.1 L52.3,58.1 L56.3,59.1 L60.4,59.9 L64.4,60.7 L68.4,61.4 L72.5,62.0 L76.5,62.6 L80.6,63.1 L84.6,63.6 L88.6,64.0 L92.7,64.4 L96.7,64.8 L100.8,65.1 L104.8,65.3 L108.8,65.6 L112.9,65.8 L116.9,66.0 L121.0,66.2 L125.0,66.4 L129.0,66.6 L133.1,66.7 L137.1,66.8 L141.2,66.9 L145.2,67.0 L149.2,67.1 L153.3,67.2 L157.3,67.3 L161.4,67.4 L165.4,67.4 L169.4,67.5 L173.5,67.5 L177.5,67.6 L181.6,67.6 L185.6,67.6 L189.6,67.7 L193.7,67.7 L197.7,67.7 L201.8,67.8 L205.8,67.8 L209.8,67.8 L213.9,67.8 L217.9,67.8 L222.0,67.9 L226.0,67.9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><path d=\"M24.0,108.0 L28.0,104.2 L32.1,100.7 L36.1,97.5 L40.2,94.7 L44.2,92.1 L48.2,89.8 L52.3,87.7 L56.3,85.8 L60.4,84.1 L64.4,82.6 L68.4,81.2 L72.5,79.9 L76.5,78.8 L80.6,77.7 L84.6,76.8 L88.6,75.9 L92.7,75.2 L96.7,74.5 L100.8,73.9 L104.8,73.3 L108.8,72.8 L112.9,72.3 L116.9,71.9 L121.0,71.5 L125.0,71.2 L129.0,70.9 L133.1,70.6 L137.1,70.4 L141.2,70.1 L145.2,69.9 L149.2,69.7 L153.3,69.6 L157.3,69.4 L161.4,69.3 L165.4,69.2 L169.4,69.1 L173.5,69.0 L177.5,68.9 L181.6,68.8 L185.6,68.7 L189.6,68.6 L193.7,68.6 L197.7,68.5 L201.8,68.5 L205.8,68.4 L209.8,68.4 L213.9,68.3 L217.9,68.3 L222.0,68.3 L226.0,68.3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"68\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/><text x=\"224\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c0\u2c7c = 1/\u03bc\u2c7c\u2c7c</text><text x=\"124\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">first race a win</text><text x=\"124\" y=\"98\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">first race not a win</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">P\u1d62\u2c7c\u207d\u207f\u207e \u2192 \u03c0\u2c7c = 1 / \ud835\udd3c[return time to j] > 0, whatever i</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Chance that Max wins race n when the first race was a win (solid) or not a win (dashed): both settle at the same level \u03c0 = 1/\u03bc, so the start is forgotten.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Convergence Theorem:</strong> For an irreducible, positive recurrent, and aperiodic DTMC, the $n$-step transition probabilities satisfy $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} P_{ij}^{(n)} = \\pi_j > 0$ for all initial states $i$ and target states $j$. The asymptotic distribution is independent of starting state $X_0 = i$.",
      "<strong>Kac's Return Time Reciprocal:</strong> The unique invariant probability $\\pi_j$ is precisely the reciprocal of the expected first return time to state $j$: $\\pi_j = \\frac{1}{\\mu_{jj}} = \\frac{1}{\\mathbb{E}[\\tau_j \\mid X_0 = j]}$. Rare states with long expected return times have proportionally tiny steady-state probabilities.",
      "<strong>Coupling Proof Paradigm:</strong> The classical Doeblin coupling runs two independent chain copies $X_n, Y_n$ until they collide at stopping time $T = \\inf\\{n: X_n = Y_n\\}$, proving $|P_{ij}^{(n)} - \\pi_j| \\le \\mathbb P(T > n) \\to 0$ exponentially."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} P_{ij}^{(n)} = \\pi_j = \\frac{1}{\\mu_{jj}} = \\frac{1}{\\mathbb{E}[\\tau_j \\mid X_0 = j]} > 0$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>The first race of the season does not matter.</strong> Whether Max won the opener or not, by mid-season the chance he wins a given race is the same $\\frac23$. That is one over the mean return time to a win: on average the next win comes $1/\\pi_W=1.5$ races later.",
    "examTrap": "Applying the limit theorem to periodic chains! If the chain is bipartite or cyclic (period d > 1), P_ij^(n) does not converge as n \u2192 \u221e but oscillates indefinitely, even though a unique stationary distribution \u03c0 exists!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Fundamental limit theorem.</b> For an irreducible, aperiodic, positive recurrent chain, $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}P^{(n)}_{ij}=\\pi_j=\\dfrac1{\\mu_{jj}}\\gt 0$ for all $i,j$, where $\\mu_{jj}=\\mathbb E[\\tau_j\\mid X_0=j]$ is the mean return time to $j$, and $\\pi$ is the unique stationary distribution (Ross 4.4; Grimmett 6.4).",
