    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "STATIONARITY TYPES",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "Strict-Sense vs Wide-Sense Stationarity",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32301\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32301)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32301)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">time t</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">X(t)</text><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><path d=\"M26.0,77.0 L30.1,58.8 L34.2,51.7 L38.2,66.0 L42.3,65.3 L46.4,66.5 L50.5,60.0 L54.6,51.5 L58.7,71.6 L62.8,85.0 L66.8,63.6 L70.9,66.2 L75.0,55.8 L79.1,77.3 L83.2,73.2 L87.2,61.2 L91.3,72.1 L95.4,52.6 L99.5,43.4 L103.6,69.4 L107.7,83.9 L111.8,73.5 L115.8,53.6 L119.9,62.5 L124.0,73.3 L128.1,71.9 L132.2,85.1 L136.2,85.5 L140.3,78.1 L144.4,71.9 L148.5,77.8 L152.6,81.2 L156.7,83.5 L160.8,76.2 L164.8,78.2 L168.9,88.9 L173.0,65.6 L177.1,62.9 L181.2,58.3 L185.2,72.1 L189.3,50.9 L193.4,44.0 L197.5,66.5 L201.6,71.3 L205.7,60.1 L209.8,54.3 L213.8,43.1 L217.9,55.3 L222.0,47.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><path d=\"M26.0,70.3 L30.1,79.9 L34.2,67.4 L38.2,81.1 L42.3,72.1 L46.4,73.3 L50.5,84.8 L54.6,75.2 L58.7,86.6 L62.8,78.8 L66.8,87.5 L70.9,91.5 L75.0,81.8 L79.1,62.1 L83.2,76.4 L87.2,80.7 L91.3,68.6 L95.4,50.6 L99.5,53.9 L103.6,62.1 L107.7,46.0 L111.8,70.3 L115.8,54.7 L119.9,66.4 L124.0,78.0 L128.1,85.3 L132.2,82.5 L136.2,62.9 L140.3,74.8 L144.4,67.0 L148.5,60.7 L152.6,66.7 L156.7,63.8 L160.8,79.5 L164.8,88.2 L168.9,87.8 L173.0,70.7 L177.1,70.2 L181.2,74.1 L185.2,66.5 L189.3,67.0 L193.4,72.8 L197.5,58.4 L201.6,53.8 L205.7,67.5 L209.8,63.3 L213.8,62.7 L217.9,49.9 L222.0,48.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03bc</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"130\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">WSS: \ud835\udd3cX(t) = \u03bc and R(t, t+\u03c4) = R(\u03c4) only depend on the lag</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"141\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">SSS: every joint law is unchanged by a time shift</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "Two telemetry traces X(t) with a constant mean \u03bc. WSS asks for a constant mean and a correlation that depends only on the lag; SSS asks for all joint laws to be shift-invariant.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Strict-Sense Stationarity (SSS):</strong> A random process $\\{X(t)\\}$ is SSS if all finite-dimensional distributions are invariant under time shifts: $(X(t_1), \\dots, X(t_k)) \\stackrel{d}{=} (X(t_1+\\tau), \\dots, X(t_k+\\tau))$ for all lags $\\tau$ and choices of time indices.",
      "<strong>Wide-Sense Stationarity (WSS):</strong> A process is WSS (weakly stationary) if: (1) $\\mathbb{E}[X(t)] = \\mu$ is constant for all $t$; (2) $\\mathbb{E}[X^2(t)] \\lt  \\infty$; and (3) Autocorrelation $R_X(t, t+\\tau) = \\mathbb{E}[X(t)X(t+\\tau)] = R_X(\\tau)$ depends only on lag $\\tau$.",
      "<strong>Gaussian Equivalence:</strong> Because a Gaussian process is completely specified by its mean function and covariance function, for Gaussian processes Wide-Sense Stationarity is mathematically equivalent to Strict-Sense Stationarity: $\\text{WSS} \\iff \\text{SSS}$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\text{SSS}: F_{X(t_1+\\tau), \\dots, X(t_k+\\tau)} = F_{X(t_1), \\dots, X(t_k)}; \\quad \\text{WSS}: \\mathbb{E}[X(t)] = \\mu, \\ R_X(t, t+\\tau) = R_X(\\tau)$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>A signal without a trend.</strong> On a steady run, if the engine vibration looks the same on lap 10 as on lap 40, with the same average and the same link between readings a tenth of a second apart, the signal is wide-sense stationary. A speed trace with its corners and straights is not.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming WSS implies SSS! Consider independent X_n with mean 0 and variance 1 where X_n ~ \ud835\udca9(0, 1) for even n and X_n ~ Uniform(-\u221a3, \u221a3) for odd n. It is WSS, but its 4th moment changes with parity, violating SSS!",
    "stmtKind": "DEFINITION",
    "statement": "<b>Strict-sense and wide-sense stationarity.</b> $X(t)$ is strict-sense stationary if $F_{X(t_1+\\tau),\\ldots,X(t_k+\\tau)}=F_{X(t_1),\\ldots,X(t_k)}$ for all $k,t_i,\\tau$. It is wide-sense stationary (WSS) if $\\mathbb E[X(t)]=\\mu$ is constant and $R_X(t,t+\\tau)=\\mathbb E[X(t)X(t+\\tau)]=R_X(\\tau)$ depends only on the lag (Papoulis 9; Grimmett 9.1).",
    "hint": "What does it mean for the engine vibration signal to look statistically the same at every moment of the run?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>SSS: every joint law is shift-invariant.</strong> Moving the whole picture by $\\tau$ leaves all finite-dimensional distributions unchanged.",
      "<strong>WSS: $\\mathbb E[X(t)]=\\mu$ = a constant mean.</strong> The average vibration level does not depend on when you look.",
      "<strong>WSS: $R_X(t,t+\\tau)=R_X(\\tau)$ = correlation by lag only.</strong> How much two readings are related depends only on how far apart they are; SSS with finite variance implies WSS."
    ],
