    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32303\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32303)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"24\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32303)\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c4</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">R(\u03c4)</text><path d=\"M24.0,97.9 L25.6,97.6 L27.2,97.3 L28.8,97.0 L30.4,96.6 L32.0,96.2 L33.6,95.8 L35.2,95.2 L36.8,94.7 L38.4,94.0 L40.0,93.3 L41.6,92.5 L43.2,91.6 L44.8,90.5 L46.4,89.4 L48.0,88.1 L49.6,86.7 L51.2,85.1 L52.8,83.3 L54.4,81.2 L56.0,79.0 L57.6,76.4 L59.2,73.5 L60.8,70.3 L62.4,66.8 L64.0,62.7 L65.6,58.2 L67.2,53.2 L68.8,47.5 L70.4,41.1 L72.0,34.0 L73.6,41.1 L75.2,47.5 L76.8,53.2 L78.4,58.2 L80.0,62.7 L81.6,66.8 L83.2,70.3 L84.8,73.5 L86.4,76.4 L88.0,79.0 L89.6,81.2 L91.2,83.3 L92.8,85.1 L94.4,86.7 L96.0,88.1 L97.6,89.4 L99.2,90.5 L100.8,91.6 L102.4,92.5 L104.0,93.3 L105.6,94.0 L107.2,94.7 L108.8,95.2 L110.4,95.8 L112.0,96.2 L113.6,96.6 L115.2,97.0 L116.8,97.3 L118.4,97.6 L120.0,97.9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32303)\"/><line x1=\"134\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"24\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32303)\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03c9</text><text x=\"138\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">S(\u03c9) \u2265 0</text><path d=\"M134.0,97.0 L135.8,96.8 L137.7,96.5 L139.5,96.2 L141.4,95.9 L143.2,95.5 L145.0,95.0 L146.9,94.5 L148.7,93.9 L150.6,93.2 L152.4,92.3 L154.2,91.4 L156.1,90.2 L157.9,88.8 L159.8,87.1 L161.6,85.0 L163.4,82.4 L165.3,79.2 L167.1,75.2 L169.0,70.3 L170.8,64.3 L172.6,57.2 L174.5,49.4 L176.3,41.9 L178.2,36.2 L180.0,34.0 L181.8,36.2 L183.7,41.9 L185.5,49.4 L187.4,57.2 L189.2,64.3 L191.0,70.3 L192.9,75.2 L194.7,79.2 L196.6,82.4 L198.4,85.0 L200.2,87.1 L202.1,88.8 L203.9,90.2 L205.8,91.4 L207.6,92.3 L209.4,93.2 L211.3,93.9 L213.1,94.5 L215.0,95.0 L216.8,95.5 L218.6,95.9 L220.5,96.2 L222.3,96.5 L224.2,96.8 L226.0,97.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><line x1=\"112.0\" y1=\"60.0\" x2=\"138.0\" y2=\"60.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32303)\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u222b</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">power spectral density</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"126\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">S(\u03c9) = \u222b R(\u03c4) e^(\u2212j\u03c9\u03c4) d\u03c4 \u2265 0,  R(\u03c4) = (1/2\u03c0) \u222b S(\u03c9) e^(j\u03c9\u03c4) d\u03c9</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">fast fading correlation \u21d2 wide spectrum</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A correlation R(\u03c4) that dies away quickly (left) and its power spectral density S(\u03c9) \u2265 0 (right), linked by the Fourier transform. The area under S gives the total power.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Wiener-Khinchin Formulation:</strong> For a WSS process, the Power Spectral Density (PSD) $S_X(\\omega)$ is the Fourier transform of the autocorrelation function: $S_X(\\omega) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty R_X(\\tau) e^{-j\\omega \\tau} d\\tau$.",
      "<strong>Non-Negativity Axiom:</strong> The PSD represents physical power distribution across frequencies and is strictly non-negative: $S_X(\\omega) \\ge 0$ for all $\\omega \\in \\mathbb{R}$.",
      "<strong>Total Power Conservation:</strong> The inverse Fourier transform evaluates autocorrelation: $R_X(\\tau) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty S_X(\\omega) e^{j\\omega \\tau} d\\omega$. Evaluating at lag $\\tau = 0$ yields the average total power: $\\mathbb{E}[X^2(t)] = R_X(0) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty S_X(\\omega) d\\omega$."
    ],
    "math": "$$S_X(\\omega) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty R_X(\\tau) e^{-j\\omega \\tau} d\\tau \\ge 0, \\qquad R_X(\\tau) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty S_X(\\omega) e^{j\\omega \\tau} d\\omega$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Hum and rattle.</strong> A vibration whose readings stay related for a long time has its power at low frequencies, like a deep hum. One that forgets itself in a few milliseconds is a rattle with a wide, flat spectrum. Engineers read the spectrum to spot which part of the car is shaking.",
    "examTrap": "Forgetting the 1/(2\u03c0) factor in the inverse Fourier transform! When integrating S_X(\u03c9) over angular frequency d\u03c9 to compute total power, omitting 1/(2\u03c0) inflates the calculated power by a factor of ~6.28!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Wiener\u2013Khinchin.</b> For a WSS process with integrable $R_X$, the power spectral density is the Fourier transform of the autocorrelation: $S_X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}R_X(\\tau)e^{-j\\omega\\tau}d\\tau\\ge0$, and $R_X(\\tau)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}S_X(\\omega)e^{j\\omega\\tau}d\\omega$; in particular $\\mathbb E[X^2]=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int S_X\\,d\\omega$ (Papoulis 10; Grimmett 9.3).",
    "hint": "How does the correlation of the engine vibration over time tell you which frequencies carry its power?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$S_X(\\omega)=\\int R_X(\\tau)e^{-j\\omega\\tau}d\\tau$ = the spectrum.</strong> Transform the lag-correlation to the frequency domain to see where the power sits.",
      "<strong>$S_X(\\omega)\\ge0$ = power cannot be negative.</strong> This follows from $R_X$ being non-negative definite.",
      "<strong>$R_X(\\tau)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int S_Xe^{j\\omega\\tau}d\\omega$ = and back.</strong> At $\\tau=0$ this says that the total power equals the area under the spectrum."
    ],
