    "highlighterClass": "highlight-purple",
    "title": "Poisson Thinning (Splitting) & Superposition (Merging)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32404\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"38\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"98\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"102\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"102\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"118\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">all incidents, rate \u03bb  (merge: \u03bb\u2081 + \u03bb\u2082)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">safety car: PP(\u03bbp)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">virtual SC: PP(\u03bb(1 \u2212 p))</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">split: independent</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">Poisson streams</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "One incident stream of rate \u03bb split by independent coin flips into safety cars at rate \u03bbp and virtual safety cars at rate \u03bb(1 \u2212 p), which are independent. Merging reverses this.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Superposition (Merging):</strong> If $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are independent Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, their sum $N(t) = N_1(t) + N_2(t)$ is a Poisson process with aggregate rate $\\lambda = \\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2$.",
      "<strong>Bernoulli Thinning (Splitting):</strong> If each event of a Poisson process $N(t) \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda)$ is independently classified as Type 1 with probability $p$ and Type 2 with probability $1-p$, the resulting subprocesses $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda p$ and $\\lambda (1-p)$.",
      "<strong>Surprising Independence of Subprocesses:</strong> Remarkably, the split processes $N_1(t)$ and $N_2(t)$ are completely *independent* random variables for all $t$, despite both originating from the exact same parent process $N(t)$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\text{Merge: } N_1 + N_2 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2); \\quad \\text{Split: } N_1 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda p) \\ \\perp \\ N_2 \\sim \\text{PP}(\\lambda(1-p))$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Safety car or VSC.</strong> If incidents come at 3 an hour and each one is independently a full safety car with chance 1/3, else a virtual safety car, the safety cars form their own random stream of 1 an hour, independent of the VSC stream of 2 an hour.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming split streams N_1 and N_2 must be correlated because N_1 + N_2 = N! For a fixed total N=n, they are dependent Binomials. But when total count N is Poisson, the random total decouples them into strictly independent Poisson processes!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>Superposition and thinning.</b> If $N_1,N_2$ are independent Poisson processes with rates $\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2$, then $N_1+N_2$ is Poisson with rate $\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2$ (merging). If each point of a rate-$\\lambda$ Poisson process is independently kept with probability $p$, the kept points $N_1\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda p)$ and the rest $N_2\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda(1-p))$ are independent (Ross 5.3; Grimmett 6.8).",
    "hint": "What happens when each incident is either a full safety car or a virtual one, and when you merge two incident streams?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$N_1+N_2\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$ = merge.</strong> Two independent Poisson streams combine into one Poisson stream whose rate is the sum.",
      "<strong>$N_1\\sim\\operatorname{PP}(\\lambda p)$ = thin.</strong> Keep each incident independently with chance $p$; the kept stream is Poisson with rate $\\lambda p$.",
      "<strong>$N_1\\perp N_2$ = the split streams are independent.</strong> Knowing how many full safety cars came out tells you nothing about the virtual ones."
    ],
