    "title": "Non-Homogeneous Poisson Processes (NHPP)",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32405\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32405)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32405)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">race time t</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03bb(t)</text><path d=\"M28.0,87.8 L31.3,87.6 L34.5,87.3 L37.8,86.7 L41.1,85.8 L44.3,84.4 L47.6,82.4 L50.9,79.6 L54.1,75.9 L57.4,71.4 L60.7,66.2 L63.9,60.6 L67.2,54.9 L70.5,49.6 L73.7,45.5 L77.0,42.8 L80.3,42.0 L83.5,43.1 L86.8,46.1 L90.1,50.4 L93.3,55.8 L96.6,61.5 L99.9,67.1 L103.1,72.2 L106.4,76.6 L109.7,80.1 L112.9,82.7 L116.2,84.7 L119.5,86.0 L122.7,86.8 L126.0,87.3 L129.3,87.6 L132.5,87.8 L135.8,87.9 L139.1,87.8 L142.3,87.7 L145.6,87.4 L148.9,86.8 L152.1,85.7 L155.4,84.0 L158.7,81.4 L161.9,77.9 L165.2,73.5 L168.5,68.7 L171.7,64.1 L175.0,60.4 L178.3,58.3 L181.5,58.2 L184.8,60.2 L188.1,63.8 L191.3,68.4 L194.6,73.3 L197.9,77.6 L201.1,81.2 L204.4,83.9 L207.7,85.7 L210.9,86.8 L214.2,87.4 L217.5,87.7 L220.7,87.9 L224.0,88.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1.1\"/><polygon points=\"60.0,67.3 63.0,62.2 66.0,56.9 69.0,51.9 72.0,47.5 75.0,44.2 78.0,42.4 81.0,42.1 84.0,43.4 87.0,46.3 90.0,50.3 93.0,55.2 96.0,60.4 99.0,65.7 102.0,70.5 105.0,74.8 108.0,78.4 111.0,81.3 114.0,83.4 117.0,85.0 120.0,86.1 120.0,108.0 60.0,108.0\" fill=\"#d9dee7\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><line x1=\"60.0\" y1=\"106.0\" x2=\"60.0\" y2=\"110.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">s</text><line x1=\"120.0\" y1=\"106.0\" x2=\"120.0\" y2=\"110.0\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">s + t</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"12\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">rate is high on lap 1 and late in the race</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">m(t) = \u222b\u2080\u1d57 \u03bb(u) du;  N(s+t) \u2212 N(s) ~ Poisson(m(s+t) \u2212 m(s))</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">shaded area</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">= expected incidents</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "A rate curve \u03bb(t) with a bump at the start and another late on. The expected number of incidents in a window is the shaded area m(s+t) \u2212 m(s), and the count is Poisson with that mean.",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Time-Varying Intensity:</strong> An NHPP relaxes stationary increments by allowing instantaneous arrival rate $\\lambda(t) \\ge 0$ to vary with time: $\\mathbb P(N(t+h) - N(t) = 1) = \\lambda(t) h + o(h)$ with independent increments.",
      "<strong>Mean Value Function:</strong> The cumulative expected arrivals over $[0, t]$ is given by $m(t) = \\int_0^t \\lambda(s) ds$. The number of arrivals in any interval $(s, t]$ follows a Poisson distribution with mean $m(t) - m(s)$: $N(t) - N(s) \\sim \\text{Poisson}\\left(\\int_s^t \\lambda(u) du\\right)$.",
      "<strong>Time-Warping Equivalence:</strong> By transforming the time axis via $\\tau = m(t)$, any NHPP can be transformed into a standard homogeneous Poisson process of unit rate $\\lambda = 1$, enabling simulation via the inverse transform method."
    ],
    "math": "$$m(t) = \\int_0^t \\lambda(s) ds, \\qquad N(t+s) - N(s) \\sim \\text{Poisson}(m(t+s) - m(s))$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Lap-one chaos.</strong> More incidents happen on the opening lap than in the middle of the race. The expected number in the first ten minutes is the area under the rate curve there, and the count is still Poisson.",
    "examTrap": "Using exponential inter-arrival times for NHPP! Because rate \u03bb(t) changes over time, inter-arrival intervals are *neither* identically distributed nor exponential. Only increments over fixed time windows remain Poisson.",
    "stmtKind": "DEFINITION",
    "statement": "<b>Non-homogeneous Poisson process.</b> With a rate function $\\lambda(t)\\ge0$, let $m(t)=\\int_0^t\\lambda(u)\\,du$. Then $N(t+s)-N(s)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}\\bigl(m(t+s)-m(s)\\bigr)$, and counts on disjoint intervals are independent (Ross 5.4; Grimmett 6.13).",
    "hint": "How do you count incidents when the rate changes during the race?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$\\lambda(t)$ = the rate at time $t$.</strong> High on lap 1 when the whole field is packed together, lower in the calm middle, up again late in the race.",
      "<strong>$m(t)=\\int_0^t\\lambda(u)du$ = the mean count.</strong> The expected number of incidents up to $t$ is the area under the rate curve.",
      "<strong>$N(t+s)-N(s)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(m(t+s)-m(s))$ = Poisson on any window.</strong> The mean over a window is the area under the rate over that window."
    ],
