    "subject": "StoMA",
    "topicId": "25_Random_Walk_Brownian_Motion_and_Gaussian_Processes",
    "topicTitle": "25 \u00b7 Random Walk & Brownian Motion",
    "cardNumber": "01",
    "category": "POLYA'S THEOREM",
    "highlighterClass": "highlight-cyan",
    "title": "P\u00f3lya\u2019s Random Walk Recurrence Theorem",
    "drawingSvg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 240 145\" class=\"w-full h-full\"><defs><marker id=\"arr-32501\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"6\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"5\" markerHeight=\"5\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M 0 1.5 L 8 5 L 0 8.5 z\" fill=\"#000000\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"64\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32501)\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"64\" x2=\"24\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" marker-end=\"url(#arr-32501)\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#000000\">lap n</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"#000000\">gap to rival</text><path d=\"M26.0,64.0 L29.3,67.4 L32.5,70.8 L35.8,74.2 L39.1,77.6 L42.3,81.0 L45.6,84.4 L48.9,81.0 L52.1,77.6 L55.4,81.0 L58.7,84.4 L61.9,87.8 L65.2,84.4 L68.5,81.0 L71.7,77.6 L75.0,81.0 L78.3,84.4 L81.5,81.0 L84.8,77.6 L88.1,74.2 L91.3,77.6 L94.6,81.0 L97.9,77.6 L101.1,81.0 L104.4,77.6 L107.7,81.0 L110.9,77.6 L114.2,74.2 L117.5,77.6 L120.7,74.2 L124.0,70.8 L127.3,74.2 L130.5,77.6 L133.8,74.2 L137.1,77.6 L140.3,81.0 L143.6,84.4 L146.9,81.0 L150.1,84.4 L153.4,87.8 L156.7,91.2 L159.9,94.6 L163.2,91.2 L166.5,87.8 L169.7,84.4 L173.0,81.0 L176.3,77.6 L179.5,74.2 L182.8,77.6 L186.1,74.2 L189.3,77.6 L192.6,74.2 L195.9,70.8 L199.1,74.2 L202.4,70.8 L205.7,67.4 L208.9,64.0 L212.2,67.4 L215.5,64.0 L218.7,67.4 L222.0,64.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"7\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u03a3 \u2119(back at 0 at step 2n) = \u221e  \u21d4  d = 1 or 2: recurrent</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"7.5\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">\u2211 n^(\u2212d/2) &lt; \u221e  \u21d4  d \u2265 3: transient</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"6.8\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-style=\"normal\" font-weight=\"normal\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000000\">level with 1 or 2 rivals again for sure; with 3, maybe never</text></svg>",
    "drawingCaption": "The gap as a random walk, lap by lap, and its returns to 0. The return chance at lap 2n is of order n^(\u2212d/2): the sum diverges for d = 1, 2 (recurrent) and converges for d \u2265 3 (transient).",
    "coreTranslation": [
      "<strong>Recurrence Criterion:</strong> By the Borel-Cantelli lemma, state 0 is recurrent if and only if the expected number of returns is infinite: $\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty P_{00}^{(2n)} = \\infty$.",
      "<strong>Dimensional Asymptotics:</strong> By the local central limit theorem, return probabilities scale as $P_{00}^{(2n)} \\sim C_d n^{-d/2}$. In 1D: $\\sum n^{-1/2} = \\infty$ (recurrent). In 2D: $\\sum n^{-1} = \\infty$ (recurrent). In 3D: $\\sum n^{-3/2} \\lt  \\infty$ converges, proving transience!",
      "<strong>P\u00f3lya's Escape Probability:</strong> On $\\mathbb{Z}^3$, the probability of ever returning to the origin is strictly less than 1: $f_{00} \\approx 0.3405$. A 3D walker escapes to infinity with probability $\\approx 65.95\\%$."
    ],
    "math": "$$\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty P_{00}^{(2n)} = \\infty \\iff d \\in \\{1, 2\\} \\ (\\text{Recurrent}); \\qquad \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty n^{-d/2} \\lt  \\infty \\iff d \\ge 3 \\ (\\text{Transient})$$",
    "personalConnection": "<strong>Back level with the rival.</strong> If Max's gap to one rival moves a random step each lap, it is sure to hit zero again, though it can take very long. The same holds for the pair of gaps to two rivals. With three rivals' gaps tracked at once, all three may never be zero together again.",
    "examTrap": "Assuming 2D random walks return quickly because they are recurrent! The 2D return probability is 1, but expected return time \u03bc_00 = \u221e (null recurrent); the walk wanders enormously before returning!",
    "stmtKind": "THEOREM",
    "statement": "<b>P\u00f3lya's recurrence theorem.</b> The simple symmetric random walk on $\\mathbb Z^d$ satisfies $\\sum_{n\\ge1}P^{(2n)}_{00}=\\infty$ iff $d\\in\\{1,2\\}$, in which case it is recurrent. For $d\\ge3$, $P^{(2n)}_{00}\\asymp n^{-d/2}$ is summable ($\\sum n^{-d/2}\\lt \\infty$) and the walk is transient (Grimmett 5.10; Ross 4.3).",
    "hint": "If the gap to a rival goes up or down a bit each lap at random, are they sure to be level again? What about with two or three rivals at once?",
    "breakdown": [
      "<strong>$P^{(2n)}_{00}\\asymp n^{-d/2}$ = return chance at step $2n$.</strong> The gaps are all back to zero at lap $2n$ with chance of order $n^{-d/2}$, where $d$ is how many gaps you track.",
      "<strong>$\\sum n^{-d/2}=\\infty$ for $d\\le2$ = recurrent.</strong> The exponent $d/2\\le1$ gives a divergent sum: the cars are level again infinitely often.",
      "<strong>$\\sum n^{-d/2}\\lt \\infty$ for $d\\ge3$ = transient.</strong> The sum converges, so with positive chance the gaps never all close again."
    ],
    "textbookImg": null,
